      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from Woodstock, England, chosen by Ray, whose ancestors had settled there before moving to America.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodstock%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1870, Ray built the first depot for the newly constructed Alabama‑Chattanooga Railroad, turning Woodstock into a railroad hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodstock%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A jug factory sprang up in Woodstock to exploit the abundant fire clay, producing bricks, jugs, pots, and churns.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodstock%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Woodstock’s ironworks mined brown iron ore from Roupes Creek, feeding foundries that produced goods for the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woodstock%2C_Alabama",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "By the late 19th century, up to eight trains a day stopped in Woodstock, carrying coal, iron ore, cotton, and mail.",
